            "text": "19: name_of_scripted_value = {\n20: \t# Mathematical operations\n21: \tadd = number/scripted value/scope.something\n22: \tsubtract = ...\n23: \tmultiply = ...\n24: \tdivide = ... # Be careful not to divide by 0\n25: \tmodulo = ...\n26: \t\n27: \tvalue = ... # Sets the value to this number"
          },
          {
            "start": 75,
            "end": 92,
            "text": "75: Example:\n76: \tadd_gold = {\n77: \t\tvalue = gold\n78: \t\tmultiply = { # Yes, you can even inline them in the mathematical operators\n79: \t\t\tvalue = 1\n80: \t\t\tmultiply = 0.5\n81: \t\t}\n82: \t}\n83: \t\n84: === Chaining ===\n85: You can reference named script formulas using scope chaining. For example, if you were to define this formula:\n86: \texample_age = {\n87: \t\tvalue = age\n88: \t}\n89: Then the following would work:\n90: \tadd_gold = {\n91: \t\tvalue = mother.example_age\n92: \t}"
